Memory card: file numbers start at 0, the lowest free number is reused, three files at most.

What it fixes: the runtime's open handed out `g_mcNextFd++` starting at 1 and only counting up, with room for
32 files. The console's memory-card manager as this game ships it (disc/MODULES/MCMAN.IRX) keeps a table of three
entries of 0x30 bytes (the loops over it run `slti ..., 3` with a 0x30 stride) and the open function scans it
from entry 0 and returns the index of the first free one; with none free it returns -7
(sceMcResUpLimitHandle, which the runtime already has as kMcResultUpLimitHandle). So the first open of the
game's save gives file number 0, and the game writes to 0. With the runtime's 1 that write answered -4
(unknown file number) and the save left a 0-byte file (docs/journal/022).
Now: the open returns the lowest free number from 0, a close frees its number (g_mcNextFd, shown by the debug
panel, is kept as "the number the next open would get": 0 at start and at sceMcInit/sceMcEnd, recomputed after
every open and close and after the files of a port are closed), and an open with three files in use fails with -7.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/mc_file_numbers_test.cpp calls the handlers as the guest does (mkdir, chdir,
open with 0x202, sync, write 992 bytes to number 0, ...). On the tree without this patch: 10 checks fail (the first
open gives 1, the write to 0 returns -4, the file has 0 bytes); with it: all checks pass.

Based on no other project's work.
